{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"THE COMMENT CEILING IS NOW ESTABLISHED FROM PRIMARY SOURCES. AC4 can be satisfied by DOCUMENTING\nPROVENANCE rather than by measurement or by marking the binding UNVERIFIED — and the working\nhypothesis that motivated the doubt was REFUTED, which changes what this story should do.\n\nTHE HYPOTHESIS I ASKED TO BE TESTED, AND ITS REFUTATION. I suspected 32767 was the limit for TEXT\nCUSTOM FIELDS, misapplied to comments, with the real comment body stored as a CLOB and effectively\nunbounded. The storage half is CORRECT — jiraaction.actionbody is type 'extremely-long' in\nentitymodel.xml (LONGTEXT on MySQL, CLOB on Oracle; Atlassian's Jira 7.9.2 schema PDF). But the\ncolumn is NOT the binding constraint: enforcement happens at the APPLICATION layer before the insert\n(CommentBodyCharacterLimitExceededException / IssueFieldsCharacterLimitExceededException).\n\nDECISIVE SOURCE — Jira's own jpm.xml, which defines advanced-settings defaults:\n    key: jira.text.field.character.limit\n    description: 'The maximum number of characters to be entered for a single field.\n                  Affects Description, Environment, Comments and Text custom fields.\n                  0 means unlimited.'\n    default-value: 32767\nSo Comments and Text custom fields are governed by ONE property and therefore THE SAME NUMBER, by\ndesign. They were never two limits that got conflated. Corroborated by JRASERVER-28519\n(Resolved/Fixed, fix versions 6.4.1 / 7.0.0): 'starting with JIRA 7.0 and Cloud,\njira.text.field.character.limit will be set to 32767 by default.' Jira 6.x jpm.xml carries the same\nkey with default 0 and already listed Comments in scope. The DC admin page 'Configuring advanced\nsettings' confirms the property, its field scope, 0 = unlimited and max 2147483647 — but note it\ndoes NOT state the default; the default comes from jpm.xml and JRASERVER-28519.\n\nSO: 32767 IS THE CORRECT DC COMMENT CEILING FOR STOCK JIRA >= 7.0. Binding comment and description\nto one constant is, unusually, structurally faithful to how DC actually works. This story must NOT\nchange the limit, and must NOT mark it UNVERIFIED — it is verified, and verified as correct for the\ndefault case. AC4 is met by recording THIS provenance (the property name + JRASERVER-28519) at the\nbinding, replacing an assumption with a citation.\n\nWHAT REMAINS A REAL DEFECT, IN THE SAME DIRECTION I SUSPECTED BUT BY A NARROWER PATH: on DC the\nlimit is ADMIN-CONFIGURABLE (0-2147483647) and rebar hardcodes the default. On an instance where an\nadmin raised it or set 0, rebar truncates comments Jira would have accepted in full. That is data\nloss. It is documented and not rare — JRASERVER-64351 has 86 votes from people hitting the ceiling.\nFiled separately rather than folded in here, per this story's own scope rule that a limit/behaviour\nchange is its own ticket.\n\nTWO SECONDARY FINDINGS THE RESEARCH SURFACED, both latent traps rather than live bugs:\n 1. THE CONSTANT ASSERTS A FALSE PROVENANCE. It is named WIKI_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT and lives in\n    rich_text.py, and the comment at jira_family/rich_text.py:56-60 attributes the number to Cloud\n    comment limits — which REVERSES the actual causality. On DC the number comes from\n    jira.text.field.character.limit, which covers descriptions AND comments.\n 2. sanitize_comment REUSES fit_outbound, THE DESCRIPTION FITTER. Behaviour is correct today because\n    that fitter is a plain character truncation. But Cloud demonstrates exactly why the coupling is\n    dangerous: Cloud's description fitter is ADF-SIZE-based (_ADF_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT = 32000 ADF chars,\n    fitted empirically because text_to_adf inflates — a 32767-char description was observed\n    serializing to ~50,566 ADF chars) and would be catastrophically wrong applied to comments. Any\n    future change to the DC description fitter silently retargets comments.\n\nWHY CLOUD HAS A SEPARATE comment_limits MODULE — this corrects an assumption in this ticket's\nContext. It is NOT because Cloud's comment ceiling differs numerically from its description ceiling;\nboth derive from 32767. It is because the UNITS differ: Cloud comments are limited in PLAIN chars\n(_JIRA_COMMENT_MAX_CHARS = 32767, a hard non-configurable Cloud limit — JRACLOUD-63007 asking to make\nit configurable was closed Won't Do) while Cloud descriptions are limited in ADF-SERIALIZED size. DC\nhas no ADF inflation, which is why ONE constant genuinely suffices there. So DC's single-constant\nbinding is not a shortcut Cloud avoided; it is correct for DC's actual text format.\n\nFIELD COMPARISON (evidence that hardcoding is the norm, so this is hardening, not a defect class):\nclient libraries unanimously do NOT cap comments — pycontribs/jira, andygrunwald/go-jira (both cloud\nand onpremise), go-jira CLI, atlassian-python-api all post the body verbatim; '32767' returns zero\nhits in each. Sentry's jira_server client posts comments raw while explicitly truncating SUMMARY to\n255 — so it considered field limits and guarded only summary. Application-level sync tools DO cap at\n32767: carrier-io/dusty annotates its constant '# This is jira.text.field.character.limit default\nvalue', an independent implementer tracing the number to the same property. RedHatQE/newa is the only\nproject found that branches on deployment type (32000 Cloud / 65000 Server) and even it guesses.\nNO surveyed project queries the live limit. Atlassian's own JCMA truncates at 32767 and LOGS it\n(MIG-1356), framing it as the Cloud target's limit and contrasting Server's configurability.\n\nNOT ESTABLISHED, recorded so nobody assumes it: whether /rest/api/2/application-properties exposes\njira.text.field.character.limit to a non-admin PAT (any read-the-live-limit remediation depends on\nthis and must be TESTED, not assumed); whether the default is still 32767 in DC 9/10/11 (the jpm.xml\nevidence read is from 7.1.9 — no primary source found for 10.x/11.x); and whether DC's REST v2\ncomment endpoint runs the same validator as the web UI (the exception class names imply shared\nvalidation, but the DC REST comment resource was not read)."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785691141519732001,"uuid":"f963d328-72b0-4c6b-b8f0-f620c44dce22"}